<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>France 24 - International breaking news, top stories and headlines</title><description>Breaking news and world news from France 24 on Business, Sports, Culture. Video news. News from the US, Europe, Asia Pacific, Africa, Middle East, America</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/</link><lastBuildDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 06:52:11 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Americas</category><title>Midterms live: US voters head to polls to determine control of Congress for Trump’s remaining term</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261103-live-americans-head-to-the-polls-to-vote-in-us-midterm-elections</link><description>
US voters head to the polls Tuesday to vote in the US midterm elections with Democrats widely projected to retake control of the House, though control of the Senate remains up in the air. They will need to gain three seats in the House and four in the Senate to overtake the Republican majority. Losing either will affect President Donald Trump's ability to pass legislation and act as a referendum on his second term. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f883627e-d642-11f1-bf43-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-31T033022Z-272937822-RC2SDOAFQW00-RTRMADP-3-USA-ELECTION-ALASKA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f883627e-d642-11f1-bf43-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-31T033022Z-272937822-RC2SDOAFQW00-RTRMADP-3-USA-ELECTION-ALASKA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dc914a3e-d711-11f1-86cb-bfbbcbd756b8</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 05:45:33 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f883627e-d642-11f1-bf43-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-31T033022Z-272937822-RC2SDOAFQW00-RTRMADP-3-USA-ELECTION-ALASKA.jpg">© Emily Mesner, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>CPJ: 'Attacks on journalists almost always precede attacks on other rights to broader population'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-cpj-attacks-on-journalists-almost-always-precede-attacks-on-other-rights-to-broader-population</link><description>
Annette Young is pleased to welcome Jodie Ginsberg, Chief Executive Officer of the Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ). The killing of journalists is not simply an assault on those who report the news, explains Ginsberg. It is an attack on the public's right to know, on institutional accountability and, ultimately, on the democratic foundations of society. As the global death toll among media workers continues to rise, a deeper crisis is emerging: the normalization of harassment, intimidation and violence against journalists and the persistent failure to hold perpetrators accountable. “If killers of journalists are allowed to literally get away with murder, that creates the conditions in which we see more and more of those attacks,” warns Ginsberg.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a991cc94-d6d0-11f1-ade7-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-131437-132031-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a991cc94-d6d0-11f1-ade7-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-131437-132031-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">af183da6-d6d0-11f1-bae2-4d80a1b698eb</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 05:02:12 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>US strikes Iran's Qeshm Island as Trump signals progress in talks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261103-us-strikes-iran-qeshm-island-as-trump-signals-progress-in-talks</link><description>
The United States reportedly struck Iran's Qeshm Island on Monday, even as President Donald Trump signalled progress in negotiations with Tehran to end the eight-month-old conflict. The reported attack was the latest in a series of limited strikes Washington has secretly carried out in recent weeks.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3748a8b2-a186-11f1-a403-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225375678305.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3748a8b2-a186-11f1-a403-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225375678305.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">34dc42fe-d72e-11f1-ac19-53b1d90820c2</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 02:28:07 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3748a8b2-a186-11f1-a403-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225375678305.jpg">© Amirhosein Khorgooi, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>European force to hold first Ukraine support exercise in Poland</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-european-force-to-hold-first-ukraine-support-exercise-in-poland</link><description>
Around 2,800 troops from 10 European countries will take part in a military exercise in southeastern Poland from November 9 to 22, testing a multinational force designed to support Ukraine after a potential ceasefire. Led by France and Britain, the force aims to bolster Ukraine's security without taking on an offensive role.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e7960aa8-d727-11f1-94d9-975020f22ee2</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 01:43:00 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg">© Philippe Magoni, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russian strikes kill at least 26 in Ukraine after war's longest ceasefire ends</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-russian-strikes-kill-26-in-ukraine-after-longest-ceasefire-ends</link><description>
Russia killed at least 26 people in strikes on a village in southern Ukraine on Monday, including civilians collecting humanitarian aid, emergency workers and police officers. The attack came hours after a rare ceasefire ended, extinguishing hopes of progress towards peace.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8385d53c-d724-11f1-b9fb-a5172ee7d599</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 01:18:44 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>North Korea rejects US allegations over $6 billion Binance crypto heist</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261102-north-korea-rejects-us-allegations-over-6-billion-binance-crypto-heist</link><description>
North Korea on Tuesday dismissed US allegations that it was behind a $6 billion cryptocurrency theft from Binance as "absurd rhetoric", accusing Washington of seeking to portray the country as a cybercriminal state and warning that US hostility could affect future diplomatic engagement.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8f8ad8a0-cdb0-11f1-9dd8-4da0d2c6855d/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T042855Z-1116982708-RC2SYNASEWEO-RTRMADP-3-NORTHKOREA-SOUTHKOREA-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8f8ad8a0-cdb0-11f1-9dd8-4da0d2c6855d/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T042855Z-1116982708-RC2SYNASEWEO-RTRMADP-3-NORTHKOREA-SOUTHKOREA-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">008923a2-d718-11f1-9b4c-999dd8670832</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 23:49:10 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8f8ad8a0-cdb0-11f1-9dd8-4da0d2c6855d/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T042855Z-1116982708-RC2SYNASEWEO-RTRMADP-3-NORTHKOREA-SOUTHKOREA-1.jpg">© Jorge Silva, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>A propos</category><title>Emergency alerts hacking 'designed to undermine trust in government', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261102-emergency-alerts-hacking-designed-to-undermine-trust-in-government-expert-says</link><description>
Emergency alert systems in Russia and Ukraine were hit in cyber attacks overnight, with fake warnings of possible nuclear strikes broadcast to users in both countries. The systems have since been brought back under control, though much remains unclear about the attacks, including who was responsible. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr Melanie Garson, Associate Professor in International Security at University College London. She says that these kind of attacks "are designed to undermine faith in the government's ability to provide trust".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea04afee-d707-11f1-b1d9-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-223118-224510-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea04afee-d707-11f1-b1d9-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-223118-224510-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f68d6d32-d70c-11f1-a237-f95335add00f</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 22:30:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Emergency alerts hacking 'designed to undermine trust in government', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/emergency-alerts-hacking-designed-to-undermine-trust-in-government-expert-says</link><description>
Emergency alert systems in Russia and Ukraine were hit in cyber attacks overnight, with fake warnings of possible nuclear strikes broadcast to users in both countries. The systems have since been brought back under control, though much remains unclear about the attacks, including who was responsible. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr Melanie Garson, Associate Professor in International Security at University College London. She says that these kind of attacks "are designed to undermine faith in the government's ability to provide trust".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea04afee-d707-11f1-b1d9-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-223118-224510-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea04afee-d707-11f1-b1d9-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-223118-224510-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ef2b778c-d707-11f1-8388-091b6edfc70c</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 22:28:36 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Truth or Fake</category><title>Trump promised 'no more wars'. His foreign policy tells another story</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261102-trump-promised-no-more-wars-his-foreign-policy-tells-another-story</link><description>
Donald Trump promised an “America First” foreign policy and pledged to keep the US out of foreign wars. But since returning to the White House, his administration has been deeply involved in conflicts around the world. From the war in Ukraine and threats to Greenland and Canada, to the US intervention in Venezuela, Trump's shifting approach to the Middle East and war with Iran, Vedika Bahl examines his foreign policy record since returning to office.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/67eb27a2-d6fa-11f1-8418-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1a252fd9.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/67eb27a2-d6fa-11f1-8418-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1a252fd9.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">24ae7466-d705-11f1-9b8a-d54c41a9e709</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 21:34:10 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Vedika BAHL</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Uganda withdrawing troops from peacekeeping mission in Somalia</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/uganda-withdrawing-troops-from-peacekeeping-mission-in-somalia</link><description>
In tonight's edition,  Uganda starts drawing down troops just days after the UN ends its political mission in Somalia. Also, upcoming municipal elections in South Africa risk solidifying growing political fragmentation.  And the drought gripping Chad could be the country’s worst in more than 40 years. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/071e4c16-d703-11f1-b357-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006258-VOD-00023657.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/071e4c16-d703-11f1-b357-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006258-VOD-00023657.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">15d625ee-d703-11f1-bde7-2b1cb8998ece</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 21:24:21 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title><![CDATA[The Long Hull: Karl Bushby reflects on perseverance & 'best of humanity' on his 28-year journey home]]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261102-the-long-hull-karl-bushby-reflects-on-perseverance-best-of-humanity-on-his-28-year-journey-home</link><description>
“They say it takes a village. In this case, it took a world.” After 28 years and some 50,000 kilometres, British explorer and adventurer Karl Bushby has returned to his hometown of Hull, completing an extraordinary life journey across four continents that illustrates not only the limits of human endurance, but also the intricate relationship between individual determination, geopolitical constraints and a world of humanity. Speaking to France 24's François Picard shortly after his emotional homecoming, Bushby reflects on an undertaking that resembles a modern-day Odyssey, although the defining obstacles were often more geopolitical than mythological.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ec1df072-d6fa-11f1-aa08-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-183552-184505-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ec1df072-d6fa-11f1-aa08-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-183552-184505-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9e81c9d8-d6ff-11f1-9be8-5196f7422720</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:54:38 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title><![CDATA[The Long Hull: Karl Bushby reflects on perseverance & 'best of humanity' on his 28-year journey home]]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261102-the-long-hull-karl-bushby-reflects-on-perseverance-best-of-humanity-on-his-28-year-journey-home-1</link><description>
“They say it takes a village. In this case, it took a world.” After 28 years and some 50,000 kilometres, British explorer and adventurer Karl Bushby has returned to his hometown of Hull, completing an extraordinary life journey across four continents that illustrates not only the limits of human endurance, but also the intricate relationship between individual determination, geopolitical constraints and a world of humanity. Speaking to France 24's François Picard shortly after his emotional homecoming, Bushby reflects on an undertaking that resembles a modern-day Odyssey, although the defining obstacles were often more geopolitical than mythological.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ec1df072-d6fa-11f1-aa08-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-183552-184505-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ec1df072-d6fa-11f1-aa08-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-183552-184505-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f0fd5b96-d6fa-11f1-b3fe-494c7bb93af2</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:40:05 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>EU says fridge attacks 'deliberate attempt to disrupt' bloc's food supply</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/eu-says-fridge-attacks-deliberate-attempt-to-disrupt-bloc-s-food-supply</link><description>
Grocery stores from Sweden to Portugal were hit Monday by a suspected cyberattack on their freezer systems, in what the EU denounced as a deliberate attempt to disrupt the bloc's food chain. Supermarkets in Belgium, Estonia, Finland, Ireland, Luxembourg and the Netherlands were among at least eight countries affected in the incident, which came three weeks after a cyberattack caused mass disruption in French grocery stores. Food retailers were forced to throw out produce that had spoiled or was at risk of spoiling after refrigerator temperatures soared overnight. FRANCE 24's Dave Keating reports from Brussels.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bcd91bec-d6fb-11f1-8781-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-211244-211858-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bcd91bec-d6fb-11f1-8781-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-211244-211858-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c2660d18-d6fb-11f1-a834-a3d33105ee23</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:30:07 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Free and fair? The Midterms and the state of US democracy</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261102-free-and-fair-the-midterms-and-the-state-of-us-democracy</link><description>
How far will the president of the United States press this time if Tuesday’s Midterm elections don’t go his party’s way? From “only if the Republicans win” campaign promises to a US Justice Department that’s already gotten involved in races, will the White House weigh on the outcome? How will ballot counting go? What’s changed since his “stop the steal” rhetoric begat the January 6th, 2021 storming of the US Capitol?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c085514e-d6f0-11f1-8735-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-02T150455Z-57526818-RC29FOA6T5LS-RTRMADP-3-USA-TRUMP-ELECTION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c085514e-d6f0-11f1-8735-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-02T150455Z-57526818-RC29FOA6T5LS-RTRMADP-3-USA-TRUMP-ELECTION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f4c3dcec-d6ef-11f1-9ae5-e5117919b596</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 19:37:21 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>MAGA: Why are Trump's most loyal supporters turning against him?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/maga-why-trump-s-most-loyal-supporters-are-turning-against-him</link><description>
Is Trump losing the support of the very coalition that brought him back to power?
 
Just one day before the midterm elections, Trump's approval rating stands at 32%, one of the lowest ever recorded at this stage of a presidency.
 
And discontent is growing beyond Democratic voters. Some of Trump's most loyal Republican and MAGA supporters are increasingly torn between political loyalty and economic reality. The war in Iran, rising costs linked to the Strait of Hormuz blockade, unfulfilled “America First” promises and other controversial issues, including the Epstein files scandal, are widening the rift between Trump and his electoral base.
 
Can he regain control of the diverse coalition that makes up MAGA?
 
FRANCE 24 journalist Stella Elgersma spoke to MAGA and Republican experts to explore how the movement has evolved, what its supporters want and what is driving their discontent.
 
Watch the full youtube documentary : https://youtu.be/XkehRG77itY?si=iOfIl0PwrgyX-j0X
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e4a4e68e-d6f0-11f1-a46d-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/minia-2-3.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e4a4e68e-d6f0-11f1-a46d-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/minia-2-3.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1a78c37e-d6ed-11f1-b42b-d5308eebe08e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 19:17:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Israeli 'unity government': Efrat Shapira expects Herzog will urge rival camps to form a 'coalition'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-unity-government-efrat-shapira-expects-herzog-will-urge-rival-camps-to-form-a-coalition</link><description>
Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Efrat Shapira Rosenberg, Israeli Radio/TV Host, Columnist, Podcaster and Educator. Following a deadlocked Israeli election, “everyone is saying who they’re not going to sit with, but no one is actually saying who they will sit with.” As President Isaac Herzog prepares to consult parliamentary factions over the formation of a new coalition, the impasse reveals tensions that extend beyond the familiar confrontation between Benjamin Netanyahu and his political opponents. According to Shapira, co-host of the highly popular Israeli politics podcast Miflaget Hamakshavot, the divisions are equally pronounced within the opposition bloc supporting Gadi Eisenkot.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d205afae-d6eb-11f1-be39-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-161238-162232-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d205afae-d6eb-11f1-be39-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-161238-162232-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d82abb72-d6eb-11f1-a9fa-fb5bfea1d499</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 19:15:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russia pummels Ukraine again after rare pause in fighting in Kherson</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-russia-pummels-ukraine-again-after-rare-pause-in-fighting-in-kherson</link><description>
Intense fighting between Russia and Ukraine has picked up again after a rare ceasefire allowed for a humanitarian mission to reach the war-torn, Russian-occupied city of Oleshky in the southern Kherson region. While Russia accused Kyiv of sending hundreds of drones toward Moscow overnight to Tuesday, Kyiv said Russia had killed dozens of civilians lining up for aid in Zaporizhzhia. 
